Since the rhel9 upgrade initialzing things is a little different, use these three commands after initial log in:

$ singularity exec --cleanenv --contain --bind /data/dust/group/h1:/nfs/dust/h1/group --bind /pnfs/desy.de/dphep/online/h1/:/acs --bind /afs:/afs --bind /nfs:/nfs --bind /pnfs:/pnfs --bind /cvmfs:/cvmfs /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/containers/fs/singularity/x86_64-centos7 /bin/zsh
$ singularity exec --cleanenv --contain --bind /pnfs/desy.de/dphep/online/h1/:/acs --bind /afs:/afs --bind /nfs:/nfs --bind /data:/data --bind /pnfs:/pnfs --bind /cvmfs:/cvmfs /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/containers/fs/singularity/x86_64-centos7 /bin/zsh
$ source /nfs/dust/h1/group/britzger/singularity_centos7/groupset.sh h1
$ source /data/dust/group/h1/britzger/singularity_centos7/groupset.sh h1
$ h1lcg

This allows the environment to run h1oo code, but condor submitions do no.  So a second work terminal without running these 3 commands is needed for condor.

Command to merge root files: hadd -k -f merged.root ***.root

I keep two different folders for this analysis, one is used for actual data and the other is for MC.

Data directory: /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC

MC directory: /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC_MC

Both directories share the same file for executables: /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/bin/x86_64-centos7-gcc9-opt/

A lot of the codes (creating event lists, analysis) are largely the same, there are only a few things I have to do slightly different for data vs MC.
So these codes are copied over between the files and I make the small adjustments.  Eventually I will just have one code with a IsMC() check to account for differnces needed.

Right now, I have many different smaller codes I run and use their outputs in the final analysis, for now it helps to have things segmented in troubleshooting, but it is a little but tedious.

Relevant codes in /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC:
create_evls.C -- Code with event selections, steering is the whole 2006 data set, outputs a file I then use as input for lambdaTree/kaonTree codes
lambdaTree.C -- Code that loops over H1FindLambda candidates and further applies cuts, output is a lambdaTree.root file which is a tree, stores lambda candidates and relavnt information for separate analysis code 
kaonTree.C -- Code that loops over H1FindK0 candidates and further applies cuts, output is a kaonTree.root file which is a tree, stores lambda candidates and relavnt information for separate analysis code


Relevant codes in /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC_MC:
create_evlsMC.C -- Code with event selections, steering is the whole 2006 data set, outputs a file I then use as input for lambdaTreeMC/kaonTreeMC codes
lambdaTreeMC.C -- Code that loops over H1FindLambda candidates and further applies cuts, output is a lambdaTree.root file which is a tree, stores lambda candidates and relavnt information for separate analysis code 
kaonTreeMC.C -- Code that loops over H1FindK0 candidates and further applies cuts, output is a kaonTree.root file which is a tree, stores lambda candidates and relavnt information for separate analysis code
genMCTree.C -- Code that takes the same input as the create_evlsMC code, but then applies our kinematic range selections on the generator level and outputes a genMCTree.root Tree file.  This is for gen level counts in efficiency calculation.

All of the codes above have associated condor_submit files in the directory, in the condor_submit files you can see my most 

Then will the Tree outputs from lambdaTree.C, lambdaTreeMC.C, kaonTree.C, kaonTreeMC.C, and genMCTree.C I have several analysis root macros I run that access these trees.
The main analysis code I am using now is /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC/AnalysisQ2.root
This code does the bulk of work for the actual analysis, extraction peaks and calculating cross sections and ratios.

I launch root from /nfs/dust/h1/group/gtustin/h1oo/H1LambdaC: "root -b"
And then run the root macro: ".x AnalysisQ2.C"

This outputs an AnalysisQ2.root file that has many plots.  Most of them are the mlp peak extractions in each Q2 bin (and inclusive extractions) to help tuning the neural network.
It also outputs the Q2 distributions of the K0_s and Lambda cross sections (and ratios/asymmetries) overlapped with the 2009 paper, and the Q2 distribution of the efficiencies.  It outputs to the terminal print outs of inclusive measurements
and some useful diagnostic information.